text:
Next, rewrite remote-ships.ts to use the merged path (while keeping the ShipRender API stable for wreck/tests). ShipRender interface: change `mesh: ShipMesh` → make it work with both. To minimize breakage of wreck + tests, keep the `ShipRender` shape but add the merged variant:

```ts
export interface ShipRender {
  mesh: MergedShipMesh;
  group: THREE.Group;
  ai: boolean;
  liveryKey: string | null;
}
```

Wreck code: `createShipRender(classId, false, info.livery)`, `outer.add(ship.group)`, `setShipOpacity(r.ship, opacity)`, `disposeShipRender(r.ship)` — all work with MergedShipMesh.

Check tests that reference the ShipRender fields... ship-mesh.test.ts, and probably remote-entities.test.ts / world-manager.test.ts reference zones. Let me grep for 'zones' inside tests.

Also, WorldManager's self-ship uses buildShipMesh (keep the old API). Character mesh is fine.

Add an option for legacy (baseline) mode to createShipRender: `opts?: { merged?: boolean }`. When merged=false, build the old 7-mesh ShipMesh. ShipRender.mesh becomes a union type {classId, group, zones?}. This complicates things. Alternative: ShipRender has `merged: boolean` and two optional fields. Let me design it:

```ts
export interface ShipRender {
  group: THREE.Group;
  ai: boolean;
  liveryKey: string | null;
  classId: string;
  /** Merged (default) — one draw call + shared state material. */
  mergedMesh: MergedShipMesh | null;
  /** Legacy (pre-TASK-58 tuning) — 7 meshes + 3 zone materials. */
  legacyMesh: ShipMesh | null;
}
```

applyShipLivery: merged → recolorMerged; legacy → applyLivery.
setShipOpacity: merged → mesh.material = shipStateMaterial(stateKeyForOpacity(opacity)); legacy → zone opacities.
